 "verdict_notes": "H5 target: >= 85% of the all-thread bandwidth. Fresh 2-thread STREAM aggregate on this host today: 33.41 GB/s (p10 31.05 / p90 35.60), so the gate needs 28.39 GB/s (2.25 ns/row at q=8). Measured ols_gram_mt 2-thread: q=8 7.16 GB/s = 20.8% of the new all-thread figure (rep-spread 18.8-23.4%), q=17 6.63 GB/s = 19.5% (spread 17.2-21.8%): H5 stays RED, with error bars nowhere near 85%. Attribution: single-thread DRAM stream is only 51% of the 2-thread aggregate (time-paired median; spread 50-55%; block medians 17.74 vs 33.41 GB/s, scaling 1.88x): DRAM is NOT single-thread-saturated on this host \u2014 mt2 has real headroom, so the H5 RED is NOT excusable as a saturated memory ceiling; it is kernel-side (per-thread throughput), not ceiling-side. The decisive check: hitting 85% of 33.41 GB/s with 2 threads needs 14.20 GB/s per thread, which IS within what ONE stream thread demonstrably pulls on this host (17.74 GB/s) \u2014 i.e. the target bandwidth is physically reachable per thread; what falls short is the kernel: each mt2 thread sustains only 3.58 GB/s (20% of the 1t stream rate), an expected 4.0x shortfall, consistent with the load-port-bound analysis of ADR-0012 (2 loads + 6 broadcasts + 12 FMAs per row cannot issue enough loads to fill DRAM). mt2/mt1 efficiency is 1.90x (q=8, spread 1.73-1.98x) / 1.23x (q=17): the threading machinery itself works (q=8 scales ~1.9x), the ceiling is per-thread kernel throughput. No CFS quota exists (cpu.cfs_quota_us = -1, zero throttle events, cgroup v1 \u2014 the v2 cpu.max path does not exist), so the committed '2-vCPU quota' phrase is wrong; the honest phrasing is '2 CPUs visible to the container (cpuset 0-1), no cfs quota'. Caveat: the all-thread denominator itself is unstable on this shared host (committed 37.6 GB/s on 2026-11-20 vs 33.41 today, and 29.28-37.59 within this run) \u2014 the H5 percentage moves with the neighbor load, but no plausible denominator brings 6.6-7.2 GB/s anywhere near its 85% threshold. N is capped at 2 by the container's visible CPUs."
